WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:07.740
All right, Ladies and gentlemen, welcome to
the webinar today on the bulk conversion of

00:00:07.740 --> 00:00:11.190
gi's to Accession.Version.

00:00:11.190 --> 00:00:16.029
I am Eric Sayers and will be speaking to for
a few minutes about this topic.

00:00:16.029 --> 00:00:21.539
If there are questions we do not get to today,
we will be posting answers to the questions

00:00:21.539 --> 00:00:23.660
with the other webinar materials.

00:00:23.660 --> 00:00:26.410
You can find that at the webinar's page listed.

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:35.000
There is also a go USA.gov URL to the materials
and those will be up later today.

00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:36.660
Let's get started.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:42.250
What we'll talk about today this is essentially
the third webinar in a series of webinars

00:00:42.250 --> 00:00:46.140
about changes to sequence identifiers at NCBI.

00:00:46.140 --> 00:00:53.320
I am just switching for a moment to a command
line.

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:55.210
I will tell you what I am doing in a moment.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:01:00.150
I will start a process that I will explain
later.

00:01:00.150 --> 00:01:02.960
Off it rurns.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:06.830
We will come back to that in a moment.

00:01:06.830 --> 00:01:13.341
What I would like to do is talk to you about
a couple of different things that we have

00:01:13.341 --> 00:01:18.500
been attempting to do with GI numbers and
accessions.

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:24.000
One of the things we talked about in the last
few webinars on July 20 and September 7 is

00:01:24.000 --> 00:01:29.590
the fact we have been changing some of the
ways we are dealing with sequence identifiers.

00:01:29.590 --> 00:01:34.909
The main thing that is happening is that sequences
that have GI numbers will continue to have

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:37.790
GI numbers going forward.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:41.720
More and more new sequences will not be assigned
GI numbers.

00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:48.190
We are going to be switching to Accession.Version
as the primary identifier for sequence records.

00:01:48.190 --> 00:01:51.329
That is something everyone needs to be more
aware of.

00:01:51.329 --> 00:01:56.290
You can go back and look at those two webinars
we had before to understand more about that.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:02:01.659
I want to talk a little as introduction to
the webinar about something we talked about

00:02:01.659 --> 00:02:07.740
on September 7 which is how you can use efetch
to handle a certain number of GI numbers and

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:10.189
convert them to Accession.Version.

00:02:10.189 --> 00:02:15.170
One of the things we understand is as this
process unfolds you may need to convert GI

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:18.469
numbers in existing systems to Accession.Version.

00:02:18.469 --> 00:02:22.290
The real motivation behind what we are doing
today is that you can do many more if you

00:02:22.290 --> 00:02:23.290
want.

00:02:23.290 --> 00:02:26.540
Again to summarize the things that are changing.

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.609
We are phasing out the usage of the sequence
identifiers called GI numbers, existing GI

00:02:32.609 --> 00:02:34.319
numbers will remain indefinitely.

00:02:34.319 --> 00:02:41.389
If a sequence has a GI number now it will
continue to have a GI number.

00:02:41.389 --> 00:02:43.689
You can continue to use that for retrieval.

00:02:43.689 --> 00:02:48.219
The real issue are sequences that will not
have GI numbers assigned to them.

00:02:48.219 --> 00:02:57.719
Those sequences in particular are things like
WGS and TSA which many do not have GI numbers

00:02:57.719 --> 00:03:00.010
now in any case.

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:03.349
More and more of those will continue not to
have them.

00:03:03.349 --> 00:03:10.499
Though sequences are a problem in terms of
trying to get GI numbers currently . That

00:03:10.499 --> 00:03:17.900
problem will continue to grow as we are unable
to assign GI numbers.

00:03:17.900 --> 00:03:24.950
Currently blast FASTA databases do not contain
GI numbers anymore and you will not see them

00:03:24.950 --> 00:03:27.529
on the default web displays of sequence records.

00:03:27.529 --> 00:03:34.109
For example the flat file records and FASTA
formats do not have them by default.

00:03:34.109 --> 00:03:40.120
I should emphasize that GI numbers are still
present in records that have them; they are

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:46.669
just not displaying on the web displays and
things like the blast definition line.

00:03:46.669 --> 00:03:54.869
After March 15, of next year, none of the
formats, flat file FASTA record formats, will

00:03:54.869 --> 00:04:01.489
contain GI numbers including the GenBank and
RefSeq releases.

00:04:01.489 --> 00:04:05.199
We realize if you already have a system that
has G.I.

00:04:05.199 --> 00:04:09.239
numbers in it you will need to convert them
to Accession.Version.

00:04:09.239 --> 00:04:13.159
On September 7 and the last webinar we talked
about a way in which you can use the efetch

00:04:13.159 --> 00:04:17.579
E-utility to do this for a smaller number
of GI.

00:04:17.579 --> 00:04:21.919
So if you have on the order of several hundred
or maybe a small number of thousands you can

00:04:21.919 --> 00:04:22.919
do it.

00:04:22.919 --> 00:04:27.150
I won't talk about this in detail you can
go back to the webinar but the basic idea

00:04:27.150 --> 00:04:34.460
is you can use an API call such as shown here
to get back a list of Accession.Version that

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:39.220
conform or correspond to the inputs of GI
numbers.

00:04:39.220 --> 00:04:40.720
Including bad GI numbers.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.240
So 100 for example is no longer a valid GI
number.

00:04:44.240 --> 00:04:45.599
The record was removed.

00:04:45.599 --> 00:04:48.290
If you do that you will get a blank in your
output.

00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:51.600
That works fine if you have a small number.

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:56.680
The real question we had to address is what
if you had a large number.

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:05.949
You can do about maybe 250 or so; if you use
the HTTP POST, maybe around several thousand.

00:05:05.949 --> 00:05:12.750
Because of our policy of use of APIs, no more
than about three calls per second.

00:05:12.750 --> 00:05:17.659
If you have hundreds of thousands or perhaps
millions of GI you need to convert this is

00:05:17.659 --> 00:05:20.729
not the most practical way to do it.

00:05:20.729 --> 00:05:27.080
What other thing could we offer to allow bulk
conversion.

00:05:27.080 --> 00:05:32.009
Enter this new resource that we have now released
on our FTP site.

00:05:32.009 --> 00:05:34.310
The resource is actualy two things.

00:05:34.310 --> 00:05:38.639
It is a Python script called gi2accession.py.

00:05:38.639 --> 00:05:43.949
And an accompanying database that contains
all the information.

00:05:43.949 --> 00:05:47.669
The database is actually in binary so you
won't be able to look at it directly.

00:05:47.669 --> 00:05:55.639
You need the Python script to de-encrypt the
database for you.

00:05:55.639 --> 00:05:57.919
The script is designed to run locally.

00:05:57.919 --> 00:06:02.080
The idea is you would download this onto your
own machine.

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.249
That is great because you don't depend on
it on a network connection or API anything,

00:06:06.249 --> 00:06:09.849
you just have to get on your own machine.

00:06:09.849 --> 00:06:13.550
You need to be aware the database file is
large.

00:06:13.550 --> 00:06:17.229
It will be about 40 gigabytes when you uncompress
it.

00:06:17.229 --> 00:06:23.030
Need to have some space on the disk to contain
it but you can get it and use it locally.

00:06:23.030 --> 00:06:28.509
The database file contains all GI numbers
that exist.

00:06:28.509 --> 00:06:33.819
Including nucleotide and protein, GenBank,
RefSeq, you name it they are all there including

00:06:33.819 --> 00:06:34.819
old GIs.

00:06:34.819 --> 00:06:38.560
So GI from previous versions of records.

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:41.650
Everything's there in one place.

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:44.289
We are updating the database every day.

00:06:44.289 --> 00:06:46.360
That's useful to know.

00:06:46.360 --> 00:06:48.530
The database changes every day.

00:06:48.530 --> 00:06:53.669
If you get it on one particular day you will
get the snapshot of the database at that time.

00:06:53.669 --> 00:06:58.819
If you need to do this conversion process
again, you may want to come back and get the

00:06:58.819 --> 00:07:03.960
most recent copy of the database to make sure
you have full coverage.

00:07:03.960 --> 00:07:11.449
One thing we anticipate about this is it probably
for many folks, is a one time operation and

00:07:11.449 --> 00:07:17.169
that you already have a database of GIs.

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:22.110
One time you can do a conversion and map the
two and that is all you will need to do.

00:07:22.110 --> 00:07:26.740
Then you can continue to update your local
databases and other services.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:33.400
Is not something we envision as being an on-demand
service to be used frequently.

00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:36.259
If that's not the case for you please let
us know.

00:07:36.259 --> 00:07:42.960
That's why we designed this is one bulk download
that you can do once and get it over with.

00:07:42.960 --> 00:07:45.150
How you get this.

00:07:45.150 --> 00:07:52.370
It lives in the FTP site, genbank/livelists/giacc_mapping.

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:56.070
You go there and you find a number of different
files.

00:07:56.070 --> 00:08:06.529
One of the files will be gi2accession.py and
the other file will be this gi2acc_lmdb.db.gz.

00:08:06.529 --> 00:08:08.530
That is the thing you need to download.

00:08:08.530 --> 00:08:11.569
That is about nine gigabytes compressed.

00:08:11.569 --> 00:08:19.930
You also find a number of other files which
are older snapshots of that single database

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:21.180
file.

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:26.180
If you point to this particular file name
you will always get the most recent version

00:08:26.180 --> 00:08:27.180
of the file.

00:08:27.180 --> 00:08:32.450
That is the one to remember if you want to
go back and get an older one you can do that.

00:08:32.450 --> 00:08:33.450
[gi2acc_lmdb.db.gz]

00:08:33.450 --> 00:08:39.719
Also a useful thing to know is we have checksum
files for that database file also in the FTP

00:08:39.719 --> 00:08:40.719
site.

00:08:40.719 --> 00:08:42.479
You can see them there.

00:08:42.479 --> 00:08:46.690
You can use either one of those to perform
a checksem after you download it.

00:08:46.690 --> 00:08:50.320
It's a useful thing to do to make sure download
is complete.

00:08:50.320 --> 00:08:54.760
How do you install?

00:08:54.760 --> 00:08:57.350
First obviously unpack the archive.

00:08:57.350 --> 00:09:01.800
Then it will blow up about four fold.

00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:05.000
To about 40 gigs of space that you will need.

00:09:05.000 --> 00:09:07.550
You need a Python environment of some kind.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:09.470
We tend to use virtual environments here.

00:09:09.470 --> 00:09:12.260
There's a variety of ways you can do it.

00:09:12.260 --> 00:09:15.920
You need a 2.7 or later Python environment.

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:18.680
You need the lmdb package.

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:23.360
We put here a simple pip command that you
can use to install it.

00:09:23.360 --> 00:09:28.889
Once you get the package that's all you need
and you should be good to go.

00:09:28.889 --> 00:09:32.760
The script runs in two different ways.

00:09:32.760 --> 00:09:34.750
In one way it's interactive.

00:09:34.750 --> 00:09:39.330
The idea here is that you can just put in
a number and a number and you will get back

00:09:39.330 --> 00:09:40.330
the G.I.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:43.810
That is what I show here in the top.

00:09:43.810 --> 00:09:48.620
You run the script it prompts you for the
GI number you give it the GI number.

00:09:48.620 --> 00:09:51.610
Then it gives you a line with numbers in it.

00:09:51.610 --> 00:09:54.430
There is actually a formatting problem here.

00:09:54.430 --> 00:10:00.270
The first number is the GI, the second up
to the point one is the Accession.Version,

00:10:00.270 --> 00:10:02.760
and this 416 which got dumped up.

00:10:02.760 --> 00:10:07.930
I apologize this is the sequence length.

00:10:07.930 --> 00:10:13.910
Another way you can do it is which is probably
the more useful way, this is a useful check

00:10:13.910 --> 00:10:17.540
to see if the script runs and is it functioning.

00:10:17.540 --> 00:10:21.320
Is to give it a file of a bunch of gi's.

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:24.050
What you can do there's a number of ways you
can do it.

00:10:24.050 --> 00:10:29.100
Take a file, put one GI per line and pipe
that into the script.

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.810
It will take that and output one line that
looks like this.

00:10:32.810 --> 00:10:37.820
It will give you the GI, the Accession.Version
and the sequence length back.

00:10:37.820 --> 00:10:42.410
Okay a live demo.

00:10:42.410 --> 00:10:46.839
Hopefully we will have fun with this and nothing
terrible will happen.

00:10:46.839 --> 00:10:49.780
Let me show you - - let me show you what I
have.

00:10:49.780 --> 00:10:53.180
I am in a linux environment.

00:10:53.180 --> 00:10:54.880
I have a few files here.

00:10:54.880 --> 00:10:56.260
I have my script.

00:10:56.260 --> 00:11:00.670
Here is the gi2accession.py script.

00:11:00.670 --> 00:11:09.839
The rest of this is some Python files and
if I was simply to run the script like this

00:11:09.839 --> 00:11:11.610
it gives me a prompt.

00:11:11.610 --> 00:11:15.870
And so if I was to enter the number 42 it
gives me that back.

00:11:15.870 --> 00:11:17.170
I can keep going.

00:11:17.170 --> 00:11:25.880
If I know another GI number, it will give
me something back.

00:11:25.880 --> 00:11:31.339
If I enter something that is not a GI number
it tells me it is not found.

00:11:31.339 --> 00:11:34.780
This allows you to say does it work or not
work.

00:11:34.780 --> 00:11:39.490
And control C control D any of those characters
will take you out.

00:11:39.490 --> 00:11:43.170
The more interesting thing is if you have
a large number.

00:11:43.170 --> 00:11:45.230
Let's imagine I have a file.

00:11:45.230 --> 00:11:48.820
What I've done is prepared a simple file.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:53.360
It only has six GI numbers in it.

00:11:53.360 --> 00:11:56.139
That's the kind of file you would need as
input.

00:11:56.139 --> 00:12:02.459
Then I can have the script and I can input
that file directly into the script and I get

00:12:02.459 --> 00:12:03.810
the output.

00:12:03.810 --> 00:12:12.569
The output, 1 is not a valid G.I. 10 the accession.version,
100 is not a valid G.I.

00:12:12.569 --> 00:12:15.320
here's another one and another one.

00:12:15.320 --> 00:12:18.519
Notice that it's agnostic about whether it's
nucleotide or protein.

00:12:18.519 --> 00:12:25.680
Those of you who have been around NCBI long
enough and use the resources may realize that

00:12:25.680 --> 00:12:31.620
CAA, three letter prefix accession is a protein,
and NP underscoreis a protein.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:34.490
These guys down here are nucleotides.

00:12:34.490 --> 00:12:35.509
Doesn't tell you that.

00:12:35.509 --> 00:12:38.290
The point is it's a simple mapper between
a G.I.

00:12:38.290 --> 00:12:41.019
number and Accession.Version.

00:12:41.019 --> 00:12:44.889
How much can you do and how long does it take?

00:12:44.889 --> 00:12:51.810
Not to bore you with having to wait around
I went ahead and created a file of GI that

00:12:51.810 --> 00:12:55.839
include all the primate refseq GI's in nucleotide.

00:12:55.839 --> 00:13:00.360
It turns out there's a bunch of GI.

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:07.250
If you look at how many there are, there's
about 2.8 million of those.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:11.200
That's a reasonable number in terms of what
you may want to convert.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.880
How long does it take the script to convert
that many?

00:13:13.880 --> 00:13:17.130
In my hands it took me 10 minutes.

00:13:17.130 --> 00:13:18.819
It's a quick process.

00:13:18.819 --> 00:13:27.029
You can have hundreds of thousands and millions
of these things you will get a quick conversion.

00:13:27.029 --> 00:13:30.079
I get a whole big file with lots of things
in it.

00:13:30.079 --> 00:13:34.639
One of the things that I would point out since
I did this and I was like this is interesting

00:13:34.639 --> 00:13:38.250
it's a good thing to know there's a lot of
not found things here.

00:13:38.250 --> 00:13:44.810
What I did was I downloaded the database on
one day and did the test the next day.

00:13:44.810 --> 00:13:46.800
Most of these that you see are brand-new.

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:48.980
They are not found.

00:13:48.980 --> 00:13:56.329
When you see not found be aware it could be
that it's an old GI and its not existing anymore.

00:13:56.329 --> 00:13:58.329
Or could be that it's brand-new.

00:13:58.329 --> 00:14:04.029
Remember the database is updated with thousands
of GI every day.

00:14:04.029 --> 00:14:09.750
You want to run at the same be to make sure
you get the most accurate information.

00:14:09.750 --> 00:14:13.420
I would probably guess for most people it's
not a big issue because you are dealing with

00:14:13.420 --> 00:14:14.890
the GIs you already have.

00:14:14.890 --> 00:14:18.810
In any case be aware that's the dynamic.

00:14:18.810 --> 00:14:26.010
Let me go back to the PowerPoint after we
survived that.

00:14:26.010 --> 00:14:29.170
And finish up.

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:32.470
Some other things that will be happening in
the near future.

00:14:32.470 --> 00:14:36.490
In one of our previous webinars I believe
it was the September webinar we talked about

00:14:36.490 --> 00:14:43.199
a new ID type parameter for the E-utilities
that is almost ready and should be going out

00:14:43.199 --> 00:14:44.639
in January.

00:14:44.639 --> 00:14:51.600
That will allow E utilities to accept accession.version
as their ID and allow them to output those.

00:14:51.600 --> 00:14:57.079
If you want more details refer back to the
September 7 webinar where we discussed that

00:14:57.079 --> 00:14:58.079
in some detail.

00:14:58.079 --> 00:15:02.930
We will have much more publicity as it goes
forward.

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:08.010
Also keep in mind the March date when the
GI will no longer be available in any of the

00:15:08.010 --> 00:15:14.949
flat file or FASTA presentations; that includes
all the things on the FTP sites.

00:15:14.949 --> 00:15:22.079
I will pause here and thank everyone for your
attention and if there are questions please

00:15:22.079 --> 00:15:23.340
let us know.

00:15:23.340 --> 00:15:26.450
Okay Eric we have several questions.

00:15:26.450 --> 00:15:30.970
One question is what about the other databases
linked in NCBI, will they continue to have

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:31.970
GIs?

00:15:31.970 --> 00:15:35.470
I'm not sure I understand the question completely.

00:15:35.470 --> 00:15:38.480
We have no control about what happens to the
outside databases.

00:15:38.480 --> 00:15:42.319
If you want to clarify please type it into
the questions pod.

00:15:42.319 --> 00:15:48.180
The second question is which non-database
method is appropriate for 50-500 G.I.

00:15:48.180 --> 00:15:49.180
numbers.

00:15:49.180 --> 00:15:54.629
If you have a small number 50-500, either
way works.

00:15:54.629 --> 00:15:59.019
You can certainly use one efetch URL to do
that number.

00:15:59.019 --> 00:16:00.490
That should be fine.

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:02.660
That means you don't have to download the
database.

00:16:02.660 --> 00:16:03.939
And use disk space.

00:16:03.939 --> 00:16:07.980
I would probably say use efetch if you have
a small number.

00:16:07.980 --> 00:16:13.040
The script and the database is designed I
would say if you have more than 10,000.

00:16:13.040 --> 00:16:16.220
The last question has to do with blast.

00:16:16.220 --> 00:16:20.089
About the blast being updated to handle GIs.

00:16:20.089 --> 00:16:27.180
That's already the case with the current version
of blast which is 2.5.0+ works with accessions.

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.459
Keep in mind the G.I.

00:16:29.459 --> 00:16:32.759
numbers are present in the preformatted blast
databases.

00:16:32.759 --> 00:16:34.740
You can continue to use those as you have.

00:16:34.740 --> 00:16:40.200
And the other question is I'm not sure why
GI is changed to accession maybe the question

00:16:40.200 --> 00:16:45.600
is why we made the change.

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.970
The basic question is like why is this happening?

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:53.500
A couple things we can say is in general over
the course of time we have had two different

00:16:53.500 --> 00:16:56.209
ways of identifying sequences.

00:16:56.209 --> 00:16:57.209
One being the G.I.

00:16:57.209 --> 00:17:00.500
numbers and one being accession.versions.

00:17:00.500 --> 00:17:06.930
In fact both of these identifiers are redundant
in that they each uniquely map to a particular

00:17:06.930 --> 00:17:09.300
sequence record.

00:17:09.300 --> 00:17:15.290
Most publications in journals require accession
numbers for publication of sequences that

00:17:15.290 --> 00:17:18.740
is a more typical way referring to sequence
data.

00:17:18.740 --> 00:17:23.620
Another advantage of the Accession.Version
over the GI is as mentioned earlier many of

00:17:23.620 --> 00:17:26.230
the sequence databases do not have G.I.

00:17:26.230 --> 00:17:27.250
numbers.

00:17:27.250 --> 00:17:33.010
Important resources such as the WGS data set
and TSA simply do not have G.I.

00:17:33.010 --> 00:17:34.680
numbers at all.

00:17:34.680 --> 00:17:38.630
The only way we can refer to those sequences
is by Accession.Version.

00:17:38.630 --> 00:17:44.350
We are moving towards a point where we can
universally access all the sequence data at

00:17:44.350 --> 00:17:48.680
NCBI in a consistent manner and that would
be Accession.Version.

00:17:48.680 --> 00:17:54.330
A follow-up question I think about blast for
making the G.I.

00:17:54.330 --> 00:18:01.180
lists and things like that to limit database
access in blast so you can pick which sequence

00:18:01.180 --> 00:18:03.170
you use.

00:18:03.170 --> 00:18:07.180
There is a seqid list option that can use
accession numbers.

00:18:07.180 --> 00:18:08.781
We are working on making that option faster.

00:18:08.781 --> 00:18:11.600
One problem is the index is slower to run.

00:18:11.600 --> 00:18:14.060
We are working on making that faster.

00:18:14.060 --> 00:18:19.560
I am not sure if it will be out in the next
release, you can currently do them with accessions

00:18:19.560 --> 00:18:22.540
but they will not be as fast as the GI number.

00:18:22.540 --> 00:18:24.320
A couple more questions.

00:18:24.320 --> 00:18:30.020
It would be helpful to know what the most
popular letter prefixes on the ID that's refer

00:18:30.020 --> 00:18:31.020
to.

00:18:31.020 --> 00:18:36.330
Some of that stuff is on our website, the
refeq accessions in particular.

00:18:36.330 --> 00:18:41.750
For the INSDC, the submitted accession numbers
that's more complex and sometimes they are

00:18:41.750 --> 00:18:42.750
arbitrary.

00:18:42.750 --> 00:18:50.560
The last question that we will take is if
I submit a new sequence it will have a new

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:52.640
accession or version update of an older sequence?

00:18:52.640 --> 00:18:54.500
I think I understand that.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:19:00.160
The answer to that is, if you submit a new
sequence you will get a brand-new accession

00:19:00.160 --> 00:19:01.160
number.

00:19:01.160 --> 00:19:02.490
It will be version 1.

00:19:02.490 --> 00:19:06.500
We are continuing to assign accession numbers
because that space is huge we can continue

00:19:06.500 --> 00:19:09.470
to do it forever.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.150
I think that's all we have time for today.

00:19:11.150 --> 00:19:15.370
If you have another question that is in the
chat pod we didn't get to, we will answer

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:16.480
it in a document.

00:19:16.480 --> 00:19:19.420
I will turn it back to Eric now.

00:19:19.420 --> 00:19:20.680
Thank you, Peter.

00:19:20.680 --> 00:19:23.470
Thank you for your attention.

00:19:23.470 --> 00:19:25.830
Hopefully this will be a useful utility for
you.

00:19:25.830 --> 00:19:27.420
Give it a try and see what happens.

00:19:27.420 --> 00:19:28.540
Let us know.

00:19:28.540 --> 00:19:36.650
If you have any questions about that or problems
with that you can write to info@ncbi.nlm.nih.gov.

00:19:36.650 --> 00:19:39.910
I put some additional other points of information
up here.

00:19:39.910 --> 00:19:45.770
You can follow our blog which is NCBI Insights
in the NCBI news and other social media, our

00:19:45.770 --> 00:19:49.920
Facebook and twitter channels for upcoming
announcements about GI and accession number

00:19:49.920 --> 00:19:50.920
issues.

00:19:50.920 --> 00:19:54.310
When they will be released and what other
changes will happen.

00:19:54.310 --> 00:19:57.870
We will do additional webinars on the topic.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:20:03.900
If you are particularly interested in the
API, utilities-announce is an email listserv

00:20:03.900 --> 00:20:07.380
for where we announce such changes for the
community.

00:20:07.380 --> 00:20:09.140
You may want to sign up for that.

00:20:09.140 --> 00:20:11.450
There's a link on the slide.

00:20:11.450 --> 00:20:16.080
Certainly you can write to info if you have
additional questions.

00:20:16.080 --> 00:20:21.050
If there are no other questions, I bid you
all happy holidays and thank you for your

00:20:21.050 --> 00:20:22.680
attention and hope to see you soon.

00:20:22.680 --> 00:20:22.920
Thank you.

